Yes — as of 2025, GitHub and other systems do support automatic archiving. GitHub offers built-in workflows to auto-archive items in projects, and there are GitHub Actions that can automatically archive inactive repositories. Other platforms also provide similar automation features.

🔑 Key Points on Auto-Archiving in 2025
GitHub Projects auto-archiving

GitHub lets you configure workflows to automatically archive items (issues, PRs, drafts) that match filters such as status (open/closed/merged), reason (completed, not planned), or inactivity (e.g., no updates in 14 days).

Archived items retain all metadata and can be restored later.

GitHub repository archiving

Archiving a repository makes it read-only, signaling it’s no longer maintained. This is a manual action, but automation can be layered on top with GitHub Actions.

Archived repos still support secret scanning and can be unarchived if needed.

GitHub Actions for auto-archiving

Community-built GitHub Actions (like auto-archive-repository) can automatically archive inactive repositories within an organization, based on inactivity thresholds.

This is useful for large organizations managing many repos.

Other systems

Project management tools (e.g., Jira, Trello, Asana) also support auto-archiving or auto-closing tasks after inactivity.

Cloud storage platforms (e.g., Google Drive, OneDrive) often include lifecycle policies that auto-archive or move files after certain retention periods.

Enterprise systems may integrate archiving with compliance workflows, ensuring inactive data is preserved but not cluttering active workspaces.

⚖️ Trade-offs and Considerations
Focus vs. accessibility: Auto-archiving keeps projects clean but requires awareness so important items aren’t hidden prematurely.

Compliance: In regulated industries, archiving policies must align with retention laws.

Customization: GitHub’s built-in filters are limited, but Actions or external scripts allow more granular control.

